# tests/test_t21_ghost.bd - T21 second process.  (FILE 2 of 2)
# ALWAYS launch with &:   run test_t21_ghost &
# Companion: tests/test_t21_watch.bd (FILE 1 of 2 - the foreground measurer).
#
# ===========================================================================
# PURPOSE
#   Be the SECOND process. This file is the MANIPULATION, not the
#   measurement - nothing it prints is the result. Its only job is to exist,
#   running, while test_t21_watch.bd measures the meter and the tick rate.
#
#   Its loop body is BYTE-IDENTICAL to test_t21_watch.bd's PHASE M loop, and
#   to test_cpu_decay.bd's idle loop before that (sleep(1) / log(...get_cpu())
#   / n = n + 1) - the loop that read a rock-steady cpu = 10.0000001798557
#   for 16+ seconds when it was the only thing running. That identity is the
#   whole design: ONE copy of this loop is a known, already-measured 10.0, so
#   TWO copies running at once should read 20.0 if the meter aggregates and
#   still 10.0 if it does not. Any deviation from 10 / 20 is itself the
#   finding.
#
#   It deliberately DOES call get_cpu() and log() every iteration, exactly
#   like the watcher, so that "two processes" means precisely "2x one
#   process". A cheaper silent ballast would contribute an unknown fraction
#   of a unit and make the expected sum something other than a clean 2x.
#   (Trade-off recorded in the T21 write-up: it means this test cannot rule
#   out that two processes POLLING the meter interact differently from two
#   processes merely executing lines. A silent-ballast variant is the
#   follow-up if the numbers come out strange.)
#
# ===========================================================================
# RUNTIME
#   90 polls at ~2.0s = ~180s, which comfortably outlives one ~60s watcher
#   run (launch, `ps` check, run, `ps` check, kill) and covers two
#   back-to-back watcher runs if you prefer to do arm B that way.
#
# WHAT YOU WILL SEE
#   NOTHING in the terminal. log() from a detached script goes to the
#   background process log (web_api.txt: "or the background process log, if
#   running detached"). That is correct and expected. The real evidence that
#   this manipulation was in effect is the `ps` / `jobs` output pasted BEFORE
#   and AFTER the watcher run - without both pastes the whole arm is
#   uninterpretable. If ghost lines DO appear interleaved in the terminal,
#   report that: it is undocumented, and it would put both processes on one
#   readable timeline for free.
#
# HOW TO RUN
#   run test_t21_ghost &
#   ps                      <- confirm it is listed. PASTE THIS.
#   run test_t21_watch      <- immediately
#   ps                      <- confirm it is STILL listed. PASTE THIS.
#   kill <pid>              <- then `ps` again to confirm it is gone
#
#   ARM C (optional): launch this file TWICE before the watcher. If the
#   second `&` is refused for lack of a RAM slot, SAY SO and skip arm C -
#   that refusal is itself a clean measurement of the concurrent-slot cap,
#   a number no document in this corpus states for the player's own box.
#
# SAFETY
#   Longest unpaced stretch: 3 lines. This script paces itself once per
#   second and should never crash on its own. If it IS killed - either
#   alongside the watcher, or on its own - that is a RESULT, not a failure:
#   a kill that crosses the process boundary is the first direct observation
#   of the documented "all processes terminated" scope, and it means the
#   budget is shared. After any watcher kill, run `ps` and report whether
#   this ghost survived.
#
# IF YOU FORGET TO KILL IT
#   It stops on its own after ~180s and writes "T21 ghost done" to the
#   background log. Always `ps` before starting the next arm - a leftover
#   ghost silently contaminates an arm A run, which is precisely the
#   stray-process scenario this test exists to quantify.
# ===========================================================================

var t0 = get_unix_time()
log("T21 ghost start cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
var n = 0
sleep(1)
sleep(1)

while n < 90:
    sleep(1)
    log("T21 ghost n=" + str(n) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
    n = n + 1

log("T21 ghost done - 90 polls, never killed, t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s")
